{
  "plan": {
    "operation": "change_assumption",
    "parent_shortcut": "single_phase",
    "response_change": {
      "type": "curvature_change",
      "parent_relation": "The current target mapping makes transmission suppression depend on total infectious burden through a single rational saturation factor built from I and I_phase, so increasing later-stage occupancy only strengthens inhibition in one fixed monotone way.",
      "child_relation": "Refine the transmission-suppression assumption so that the marginal effect of later-stage occupancy on infection generation changes with stage composition more sharply than the current single rational form, yielding stronger curvature in how dI_dt falls as I_phase becomes a larger share of infectious prevalence.",
      "affected_inputs": [
        "S",
        "I",
        "I_phase",
        "saturation_strength",
        "phase_curvature_strength"
      ],
      "observable_effect": "At matched S and similar total infectious prevalence, outbreaks with higher I_phase/I show a disproportionately lower growth rate of I than the parent predicts, especially near peak and early decline."
    },
    "diagnostic_groups": [
      {
        "name": "total_infectious_load",
        "expression": "I + I_phase",
        "role": "Overall infectious burden entering transmission saturation"
      },
      {
        "name": "late_phase_share",
        "expression": "I_phase/(I + I_phase + 1.0e-12)",
        "role": "Stage composition indicator distinguishing early-growth from late-outbreak infectious mix"
      },
      {
        "name": "transmission_pressure",
        "expression": "S*I",
        "role": "Unsaturated mass-action infection pressure before prevalence-dependent suppression"
      }
    ],
    "activation_condition": "Visible when infectious prevalence is moderate to high and I_phase is a non-negligible fraction of I, so stage composition varies over the sampled rise-to-decline trajectory while transmission and recovery parameters remain fixed.",
    "train_hidden_contrast": "During training, the mechanism appears as stronger-than-parent bending of dI_dt versus I_phase/I around the approach to peak; in holdout, the same composition-dependent curvature extends further into the decline without changing the underlying closed-population setting.",
    "continuation_path": "Recover the parent law in the limit where the added stage-composition curvature control tends to zero, reducing the refined suppression function back to the current rational dependence on I and I_phase.",
    "freedom_change": "none",
    "promoted_symbol": null,
    "promotion_reason": "",
    "gap_statement": "The current model already lets later infectious-stage occupancy alter transmission suppression, but it assumes a single fixed rational sensitivity that may be too rigid to capture how symptom-linked withdrawal or repeated-contact crowding steepens as the infectious pool becomes dominated by later-stage cases.",
    "baseline_assumption": "Later-stage occupancy affects effective transmission through one fixed-form saturation denominator with a single curvature contribution, implying one shared shape for suppression across all stage compositions.",
    "scientific_mechanism": "As infection progresses through an acute outbreak, later-stage cases may withdraw from contact networks disproportionately strongly, so composition changes in the infectious pool can bend transmission more sharply than a single rational saturation shape allows.",
    "affected_symbols": [
      "S",
      "I",
      "I_phase",
      "saturation_strength",
      "phase_curvature_strength"
    ],
    "rationale": "A realistic next refinement is to relax the parent's fixed-shape stage-composition effect inside transmission suppression rather than adding a new process. The parent already encodes that later infectious occupancy reduces transmission efficiency, but only through one rational denominator whose shape is globally fixed once parameters are set. The scientific gap is that symptom-linked contact withdrawal or crowding can intensify nonlinearly as later-stage occupancy becomes a larger share of infectious prevalence, not merely as an additive burden term. The varying condition is stage composition along the observed outbreak trajectory, summarized by I_phase relative to I and total infectious load, while transmission and recovery conditions remain fixed and the same initial-condition protocol is used. Under admissible conditions with moderate prevalence and appreciable I_phase accumulation, the child should show stronger downward curvature of dI_dt at similar S and similar total infectious burden when I_phase/I is larger. This changes the scored target mapping itself, not only auxiliary trajectories, because dI_dt directly depends on how transmission is suppressed by observed I and I_phase. A single refit of existing parent parameters can shift overall saturation scale or current quadratic contribution, but it cannot generally reproduce a systematically different composition-dependent curvature across both early rise and late peak/decline if the true suppression steepens with late-phase share beyond the parent's fixed rational shape. Limitation: if I_phase remains near zero throughout observations, the refinement would be weakly identifiable, so the claimed contrast relies on appreciable later-stage occupancy within the sampled outbreak window."
  },
